  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nHeat:\n\nNot much change as dew points in the upper 60s to mid 70s provide \nthe area with an oppressive environment. As temperatures rise into \nthe 90s today and Sunday, heat indices will jump to between 100-110 \ndegrees in most locations. The hottest conditions continue to look \nto be along the James and Missouri River valleys. \n\nFor now Monday through Wednesday should see highs generally in the \n90s but with a bit less humidity. This will keep the heat index in \nthe 90s as well. Will have to keep an eye on the dew points trends \nas a few more degrees of moisture will push heat indices above 100.\n\nA wave moving through on Wednesday will likely bring slightly cooler \nair into the area so the second half of the week may not be quite as \nhot.\n\nThunderstorms:\n\nOverall a capping inversion will be in place for the area through \nthe afternoon, so development is not expected. One exception may be \nin and around Gregory/Charles Mix counties where model soundings \nhint at possibly breaking the cap. If this can happen, an isolated \nstorm with the potential for wind gusts around 70 mph are possible. \nCAPE values only running about 500-1500 J/kg with fairly weak shear, \nbut with a very dry subcloud layer. Otherwise, development should \ntake place west of the area and move in after about 6-8 pm. This \ncluster of storms will likely be a bit pulsy, but with better \ninstability and still a fairly significant sub cloud dry layer wind \ngusts to 75 mph will be likely with the potential for a cold pool to \ndevelop. Hail to the size of quarters will also be possible, but \nwind will be the main threat. Many of the latest 12z models are \njumping on this wind threat and the HRRR has been very consistent \nbringing this wind threat right down I-90 this evening.\n\nSome lingering showers are possible from midnight into early Sunday \nmorning, but are not expected to be severe and may even have trouble \nreaching the ground with a significant dry layer below. Still a lot \nof question marks regarding the severe threat tomorrow afternoon and \ntomorrow night. For now a significant amount of dry air in the low \nto mid levels should provide enough capping to avoid afternoon \nthunderstorm development. If a storm could develop, 2000 J/kg CAPE, \n30-40 knots of deep layer shear and a significant dry layer in the \nlower levels would support wind gusts to 75 mph and hail to the size \nof half dollars. Otherwise during the later evening and overnight \nhours the models are really backing off on any potential for the \narea and keeping the isolated to scattered activity to the northeast.\n\nA wave will move through the area Wednesday into Thursday morning \nand be the next chance for showers and thunderstorms. This will be \nthe next threat for possible severe storms. The main threat for \nshowers and thunderstorms should come on Thursday.",
